from ../testing import Testing
from ../tooling/cli/Plugins import PluginsCli
from ./Api import PluginApi
from ./Build import PluginBuild
from ./Capabilities import PluginCapabilities
from ./Declaration import PluginDeclaration
from ./Dependencies import PluginDependencies
from ./Inspection import PluginInspection
from ./Reach import PluginReach
from ./Rendering import PluginRendering
from ./Runtime import PluginRuntime
from ./Sources import PluginSources

export anchor Plugins:
    description:
        Plugins make the compiler extensible: they add commands to the CLI and
        Piton files to import, without changing the compiler. A plugin is
        declared in Piton and written in TypeScript, which Piton transpiles
        and runs itself in QuickJS, so using one needs nothing installed
        beyond piton.

        Two plugins are bundled with the compiler. Belay, the framework that
        compiles agent guidance, is a plugin: its constructs and adapters are
        its Piton files, and its build writes what agentic tools read. And
        @{Testing}: `piton test` and the test keyword, which builds on Belay
        for the agentic tools it launches.

    principles:
        Compiling stays pure. Everything that can be known about a plugin
        without running it is in its Piton declaration: its name, its commands
        and their arguments, its settings, what it requires, and what it may
        do on the machine. `piton compile`, and the language server as you
        type, read Piton only. A plugin's code runs only when a command it
        adds is invoked, when `piton build` or `piton check` plans its build,
        when `piton reach` asks what it reaches, and when the editor saves a
        file.

        A plugin may only do what it declares. It reads the compiled project
        freely, since that changes nothing, but every other effect, like
        running a program or writing a file, is a capability it has to list,
        and anything it didn't list is refused.

        Bundled and installed plugins are the same. A bundled plugin's files
        are embedded in the compiler instead of read from disk, and that is
        the only difference, so the design that runs `piton test` is the one
        an installed plugin uses.

    declaration: @{PluginDeclaration}
    api: @{PluginApi}
    capabilities: @{PluginCapabilities}
    build: @{PluginBuild}
    rendering: @{PluginRendering}
    dependencies: @{PluginDependencies}
    inspection: @{PluginInspection}
    reach: @{PluginReach}
    runtime: @{PluginRuntime}
    sources: @{PluginSources}
    command: @{PluginsCli}

    notYet:
        Plugins add commands, build output, the diagnostics their builds
        find, and the anchors they reach. They don't yet add renderers for `piton compile`, add
        diagnostics outside a build, or extend a command the compiler has.
        Each of these would extend the same declaration and API, rather than
        add a second way to extend the compiler.
